turning an LED on and off.

78
00:04:10,519 --> 00:04:17,120
So by the end of this session, we should know

79
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:20,560
what a microcontroller is, what STM32 is, and what ARM is,

80
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:23,160
as well as the hardware and software

81
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:24,600
we use to make our first project.

82
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:26,560
We need to fully understand

83
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:31,120
all of these concepts

84
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:34,199
before moving on.

85
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:36,199

86
00:04:34,199 --> 00:04:38,960
As I said, a microcontroller is a small computer.

87
00:04:36,199 --> 00:04:40,880
A small computer that has, essentially,

88
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:43,720
the same CPU, Flash, RAM, and other components

89
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:47,160
that exist separately inside a computer or laptop

90
00:04:43,720 --> 00:04:49,560
but are integrated into one chip in a microcontroller.

91
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:53,000
All of them are placed together in one chip.

92
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:55,759
Now let me explain what each of them does.

93
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:58,680

94
00:04:55,759 --> 00:05:00,648

95
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:03,360
One very, very important part

96
00:05:00,648 --> 00:05:05,960
that exists in both microcontrollers and computers

97
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:08,880
is the CPU.

98
00:05:05,960 --> 00:05:12,160
What exactly does the CPU do? Look,

99
00:05:08,880 --> 00:05:13,960
you write some code.

100
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:17,400
Okay, you have written it.

101
00:05:13,960 --> 00:05:20,080
Who executes that code and those instructions,

102
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:24,160
and controls their execution?

103
00:05:20,080 --> 00:05:26,960
That is the CPU. It is the processing core

104
00:05:24,160 --> 00:05:31,240
that performs these tasks for you.

105
00:05:26,960 --> 00:05:34,639
So the CPU executes the instructions of the program.

106
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:36,080
Again, the CPU executes

107
00:05:34,639 --> 00:05:38,800
the instructions of the program.

108
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:43,039
Always keep this in mind: the job of the CPU

109
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:46,280
is to process those instructions.

110
00:05:43,039 --> 00:05:49,560
Now we come to Flash, another very important part

111
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:55,120
that we use in microcontrollers.

112
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:58,039
When you write code and program it onto your microcontroller,

113
00:05:55,120 --> 00:06:01,039
that information and code are stored

114
00:05:58,039 --> 00:06:05,280
in a permanent memory called Flash.

115
00:06:01,039 --> 00:06:09,759
What does permanent memory mean? For example,

116
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:11,639
I recently completed a project for a company.

117
00:06:09,759 --> 00:06:14,560
It was a hood project, a kitchen range hood.

118
00:06:11,639 --> 00:06:18,880
It had around 2,500 lines of code.

119
00:06:14,560 --> 00:06:22,840
Those 2,500 lines need to be stored somewhere permanently.

120
00:06:18,880 --> 00:06:25,960
Why do I say permanently? Because when the power goes out,

121
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:28,319
or the device is turned off and on again,

122
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:30,560
the program must still be there.

123
00:06:28,319 --> 00:06:34,080
When there is no power, or we disconnect the hood's power,

124
00:06:30,560 --> 00:06:37,680
the program must remain stored somewhere

125
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:40,960
so that when the power comes back,

126
00:06:37,680 --> 00:06:43,520
or we turn the hood's power back on,

127
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:47,120
the program can start up again

128
00:06:43,520 --> 00:06:50,400
and perform its proper function.

129
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:53,199
If it is not in Flash memory,

130
00:06:50,400 --> 00:06:56,400
it will not perform its intended function correctly.

131
00:06:53,199 --> 00:06:59,360
So it is very important that the microcontroller

132
00:06:56,400 --> 00:07:03,199
we use has Flash memory,

133
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:06,280
so the program we write can be stored there permanently.

134
00:07:03,199 --> 00:07:08,680
The program remains there

135
00:07:06,280 --> 00:07:10,720
and is not lost when power is removed.

136
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:13,520
That is what Flash memory is for.

137
00:07:10,720 --> 00:07:16,560
I explained it with this example.

138
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:18,599

139
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:22,000
There is another type of memory that is very important

140
00:07:18,599 --> 00:07:25,360
in microcontrollers: RAM.

141
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:28,840
What is RAM? RAM is memory that we only need

142
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:31,319
while the program is running.

143
00:07:28,840 --> 00:07:33,039
After that, we do not need it anymore.

144
00:07:31,319 --> 00:07:36,560
For example, variables: while the program is running,

145
00:07:33,039 --> 00:07:39,520
the variables it needs are stored and used in RAM.

146
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:41,639
Afterward, we no longer need them.

147
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:44,520
So variables or data that we do not need permanently

148
00:07:41,639 --> 00:07:48,039
are stored in RAM,

149
00:07:44,520 --> 00:07:51,280
and when the power is turned off and on again,

150
00:07:48,039 --> 00:07:54,599
that data is erased.

151
00:07:51,280 --> 00:07:56,680
So be careful about what we put in Flash

152
00:07:54,599 --> 00:07:59,800
and what we put in RAM.

153
00:07:56,680 --> 00:08:02,520
RAM holds temporary data,

154
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:07,560
while the code and permanent data are kept in Flash.

155
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:09,120
Keep that distinction in mind.

156
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:12,879
Another very important part of microcontrollers

157
00:08:09,120 --> 00:08:15,240
is the peripherals.

158
00:08:12,879 --> 00:08:18,159
What do peripherals do?

159
00:08:15,240 --> 00:08:20,680
In one sentence, peripherals help the CPU

160
00:08:18,159 --> 00:08:23,919
as auxiliary units so that you can communicate

161
00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:26,240
with the outside world.

162
00:08:23,919 --> 00:08:29,440
For example, suppose you want to measure the outside temperature.

163
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:31,560
A sensor measures the temperature

164
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:34,360
and sends it as a signal into the microcontroller.

165
00:08:31,560 --> 00:08:37,479
Without the appropriate peripheral,

166
00:08:34,360 --> 00:08:38,880
you would not be able to do this.

167
00:08:37,479 --> 00:08:41,640
So peripherals are auxiliary units

168
00:08:38,880 --> 00:08:45,480
that help you communicate with the outside world

169
00:08:41,640 --> 00:08:48,440
and build your projects.

170
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:51,160
The peripherals available in STM32 include

171
00:08:48,440 --> 00:08:54,560
UART, SPI, I2C, timers, and others.

172
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:57,279
These are things that you can use in your projects.

173
00:08:54,560 --> 00:08:59,600
I will cover each of them one by one in the projects.

174
00:08:57,279 --> 00:09:01,720
There is also GPIO,

175
00:08:59,600 --> 00:09:03,920
which refers to our inputs and outputs.

176
00:09:01,720 --> 00:09:08,279
This part is also very important.

177
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:12,440
The microcontroller reads its inputs

178
00:09:08,279 --> 00:09:15,920
and controls its outputs.

179
00:09:12,879 --> 00:09:18,720
The GPIO section represents

180
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:21,519
our input and output pins.

181
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:24,160
The microcontroller reads the inputs

182
00:09:21,519 --> 00:09:27,160
and controls the outputs.

183
00:09:24,160 --> 00:09:30,160
That is its basic function.

184
00:09:27,160 --> 00:09:30,160

185
00:09:30,600 --> 00:09:39,040
Okay, this slide is very important.

186
00:09:34,399 --> 00:09:41,640
It explains the concepts of STM32, Cortex, and Cortex-M,

187
00:09:39,040 --> 00:09:43,519
and how they are related to each other.

188
00:09:41,640 --> 00:09:48,200
Listen carefully to this slide once

189
00:09:43,519 --> 00:09:52,440
so that you understand the relationship between these terms.

190
00:09:48,200 --> 00:09:56,079
First, there is ST. Many people call it ST.

191
00:09:52,200 --> 00:10:00,279
ST stands for STMicroelectronics,

192
00:09:56,079 --> 00:10:04,560
which is the company that manufactures STM32.

193
00:10:00,279 --> 00:10:08,040
So STM32 is a chip,

194
00:10:04,560 --> 00:10:10,240
a microcontroller manufactured by ST.

195
00:10:08,040 --> 00:10:12,600
That's the first point.

196
00:10:10,240 --> 00:10:16,399
Okay, so what is ARM?

197
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:18,360
As we said on the previous slide,

198
00:10:16,399 --> 00:10:20,160
the STM32 microcontroller contains a CPU.

199
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:22,680
There is a processing unit inside it.

200
00:10:20,160 --> 00:10:25,839
ST does not manufacture that processing core itself.

201
00:10:22,680 --> 00:10:27,600
For various reasons, including cost,

202
00:10:25,839 --> 00:10:30,000
it does not build the CPU core itself.

203
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:32,480
Instead, it obtains it from ARM.

204
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:35,880
ARM is a company that designs

205
00:10:32,480 --> 00:10:40,440
the processor cores used in microcontrollers.

206
00:10:35,880 --> 00:10:44,360
So, ST manufactures the STM32 chip,

207
00:10:40,440 --> 00:10:48,079
while ARM designs the processor core for it.

208
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:51,079
So far, ST manufactures the STM32 chip,

209
00:10:48,079 --> 00:10:54,240
and ARM provides the core.

210
00:10:51,079 --> 00:10:56,800
ARM says, essentially, "I will provide

211
00:10:54,240 --> 00:10:57,600
the core for your STM32."

212
00:10:56,800 --> 00:10:59,600
That's the relationship.

213
00:10:57,600 --> 00:11:04,720

214
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:08,600
So ARM is the company that designs the processor core

215
00:11:04,720 --> 00:11:11,680
used in STM32 microcontrollers.

216
00:11:08,600 --> 00:11:14,880
Now, when you hear Cortex-M or Cortex-MT,

217
00:11:11,680 --> 00:11:17,600
what does that mean? Let's see.

218
00:11:14,880 --> 00:11:20,639
The ARM company that designs the core

219
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:24,160
gives that core a name.

220
00:11:20,639 --> 00:11:27,079
The name is Cortex-M.

221
00:11:24,160 --> 00:11:29,120
There can be different versions,

222
00:11:27,079 --> 00:11:32,360
such as Cortex-M0, Cortex-M3, Cortex-M4, and so on.

223
00:11:29,120 --> 00:11:34,680
In the project we are using,

224
00:11:32,360 --> 00:11:40,000
we use an STM32F103,

225
00:11:34,680 --> 00:11:43,079
which uses a Cortex-M3 core.

226
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:46,480
These names exist because

227
00:11:43,079 --> 00:11:50,519
each core has a different processing capability.

228
00:11:46,480 --> 00:11:52,639
For example, in this course we are using

229
00:11:50,519 --> 00:11:55,399
the STM32F103,

230
00:11:52,639 --> 00:11:57,680
and the Cortex-M3 core inside it

231
00:11:55,399 --> 00:12:00,360
is sufficient for our work.

232
00:11:57,680 --> 00:12:04,320
That's why we use this microcontroller.

233
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:09,600
So, once again, ST manufactures the STM32 microcontroller,

234
00:12:04,320 --> 00:12:12,720
or chip,

235
00:12:09,600 --> 00:12:15,160
but it does not manufacture the processing core itself.

236
00:12:12,720 --> 00:12:18,720
It obtains the processor core from ARM,

237
00:12:15,160 --> 00:12:21,800
and ARM provides that processing core.

238
00:12:18,720 --> 00:12:24,360
In this example, the name of the processing core

239
00:12:21,800 --> 00:12:28,680
is Cortex-M3.

240
00:12:24,360 --> 00:12:30,320
These relationships are very important.

241
00:12:28,680 --> 00:12:33,480

242
00:12:30,320 --> 00:12:38,760
Pay close attention to this slide

243
00:12:33,480 --> 00:12:42,279
so you do not forget the relationship between these terms later.

244
00:12:38,760 --> 00:12:46,839
Another important point is the STM32 family.

245
00:12:42,279 --> 00:12:50,120
STMicroelectronics has several STM32 families.

246
00:12:46,839 --> 00:12:52,839
For example, STM32F0 is an economical family

247
00:12:50,120 --> 00:12:54,560
and is very inexpensive.

248
00:12:52,839 --> 00:12:57,360
Students who have student projects

249
00:12:54,560 --> 00:13:00,000
can use this type of microcontroller.

250
00:12:57,360 --> 00:13:03,120
For our training course,

251
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:06,639
we are using the STM32F103,

252
00:13:03,120 --> 00:13:08,320
which is a suitable microcontroller for this course.

253
00:13:06,639 --> 00:13:12,000

254
00:13:08,320 --> 00:13:14,920
Then we have F3 and F4, which have higher processing capabilities,

255
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:17,880
and they are used for more advanced applications.

256
00:13:14,920 --> 00:13:21,040
For very industrial and advanced projects,

257
00:13:17,880 --> 00:13:22,720
there are the STM32H families.

258
00:13:21,040 --> 00:13:25,600
Naturally, these are more expensive.

259
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:28,399
So, in general, some are cheaper and some are more expensive.

260
00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:31,199
The heavier the project,

261
00:13:28,399 --> 00:13:34,279
the more capable—and usually more expensive—the microcontroller will be.

262
00:13:31,199 --> 00:13:36,279

263
00:13:34,279 --> 00:13:38,639
Now, why do we choose STM32?

264
00:13:36,279 --> 00:13:41,000
Why do we choose STM32 instead of AVR, Holtek,

265
00:13:38,639 --> 00:13:43,800
or other microcontrollers?

266
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:48,000
First, one of the advantages is its peripherals.

267
00:13:43,800 --> 00:13:50,120
STM32 offers very powerful and diverse peripherals.

268
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:52,199
For example, it supports CAN,

269
00:13:50,120 --> 00:13:55,839
which is widely used in vehicles.

270
00:13:52,199 --> 00:13:59,800
The CAN protocol is used extensively in automotive systems,

271
00:13:55,839 --> 00:14:02,680
but some other microcontrollers may not provide it.

272
00:13:59,800 --> 00:14:05,320
There are also SPI, I2C, ADC, and other peripherals.

273
00:14:02,680 --> 00:14:08,800
The variety of peripherals is one reason

274
00:14:05,320 --> 00:14:10,639
we choose STM32.

275
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:14,480
The variety is very high.

276
00:14:10,639 --> 00:14:19,040
For example, the F1 family has many microcontrollers,

277
00:14:14,480 --> 00:14:22,440
and the F4 family also has many microcontrollers.

278
00:14:19,040 --> 00:14:23,839
So there is a lot of variety.

279
00:14:22,440 --> 00:14:25,360
Another important point is scalability.

280
00:14:23,839 --> 00:14:28,279
What you learn here can also be used on other models.

281
00:14:25,360 --> 00:14:31,079
For example, if you learn something on the F103,

282
00:14:28,279 --> 00:14:32,839
you can apply the same concepts to an F4.

283
00:14:31,079 --> 00:14:35,079
And there are also the development tools.

284
00:14:32,839 --> 00:14:37,040

285
00:14:35,079 --> 00:14:39,639
And the final point, which is very important,

286
00:14:37,040 --> 00:14:42,120
is STM32CubeMX.

287
00:14:39,639 --> 00:14:44,399
As I will explain later,

288
00:14:42,120 --> 00:14:47,639
it is a wizard that you use in your project.

289
00:14:44,399 --> 00:14:50,160
It makes your work much faster and more accurate.

290
00:14:47,639 --> 00:14:53,199
ST provides this wizard

291
00:14:50,160 --> 00:14:56,560
so you can configure your I/O and communication protocols,

292
00:14:53,199 --> 00:14:59,279
and other settings,

293
00:14:56,560 --> 00:15:01,399
without having to write every register configuration

294
00:14:59,279 --> 00:15:04,320
from scratch in your program.

295
00:15:01,399 --> 00:15:06,720
Doing everything manually is time-consuming

296
00:15:04,320 --> 00:15:09,120
and greatly increases the chance of errors.

297
00:15:06,720 --> 00:15:12,079
So these are some of the advantages

298
00:15:09,120 --> 00:15:14,160
we have with STM32,

299
00:15:12,079 --> 00:15:16,600
which is why we use it.

300
00:15:14,160 --> 00:15:21,519

301
00:15:16,600 --> 00:15:24,560
Here, a general overview has also been provided

302
00:15:21,519 --> 00:15:27,320
that you should keep in mind.

303
00:15:24,560 --> 00:15:31,240
So remember: the processor core of STM32

304
00:15:27,320 --> 00:15:33,920
is designed by ARM,

305
00:15:31,240 --> 00:15:37,920
and it is called Cortex-M.

306
00:15:33,920 --> 00:15:39,880
For the specific microcontroller we are using,

307
00:15:37,920 --> 00:15:42,120
which is the STM32F103,

308
00:15:39,880 --> 00:15:45,600
we have the Cortex-M3 core.

309
00:15:42,120 --> 00:15:47,600
This is the processor core being used.

310
00:15:45,600 --> 00:15:50,079
It is relatively inexpensive,

311
00:15:47,600 --> 00:15:53,920
which makes it suitable for our training course.

312
00:15:50,079 --> 00:15:58,199
And this is the microcontroller we are using.

313
00:15:53,920 --> 00:16:01,519

314
00:15:58,199 --> 00:16:04,319
The overall structure of STM32 is like this:

315
00:16:01,519 --> 00:16:07,560
we have the CPU, which is the processing core,

316
00:16:04,319 --> 00:16:11,880
and in this example it is Cortex-M3.

317
00:16:07,560 --> 00:16:15,560
Then we have different sections, such as I/O,

318
00:16:11,880 --> 00:16:18,680
or GPIO, for inputs and outputs.

319
00:16:15,560 --> 00:16:22,600
There are timers, SPI, UART,

320
00:16:18,680 --> 00:16:25,319
I2C, and other serial communication interfaces.

321
00:16:22,600 --> 00:16:28,639
There is RAM, which is temporary memory,

322
00:16:25,319 --> 00:16:32,079
and permanent Flash memory.

323
00:16:28,639 --> 00:16:34,199
This is the overall structure of STM32.

324
00:16:32,079 --> 00:16:38,040

325
00:16:34,199 --> 00:16:40,880
Another very important part is the course hardware.

326
00:16:38,040 --> 00:16:43,600
The most important tools we will use in our hardware projects

327
00:16:40,880 --> 00:16:46,800
include the Blue Pill.

328
00:16:43,600 --> 00:16:50,399
I think it costs around 40 or 50 tomans,

329
00:16:46,800 --> 00:16:52,560
although I will give you the current price.

330
00:16:50,399 --> 00:16:56,040
The other one is ST-Link.

331
00:16:52,560 --> 00:17:01,680
We always need these two, so you should definitely get them.

332
00:16:56,040 --> 00:17:03,600
On the Blue Pill itself,

333
00:17:01,680 --> 00:17:07,959
the STM32F103C8T6 microcontroller

334
00:17:03,600 --> 00:17:10,439
is mounted here.

335
00:17:07,959 --> 00:17:13,199

336
00:17:10,439 --> 00:17:15,280
There is also an external 72 MHz crystal

337
00:17:13,199 --> 00:17:18,240
mounted on the board.

338
00:17:15,280 --> 00:17:22,120
Whenever we want to use an external crystal,

339
00:17:18,240 --> 00:17:25,600
we can use this one.

340
00:17:22,120 --> 00:17:28,679
Then there is the bootloader,

341
00:17:25,600 --> 00:17:32,440
and there is also a reset button.

342
00:17:28,679 --> 00:17:35,280
There are four pins here

343
00:17:32,440 --> 00:17:39,200
that connect to the ST-Link, our programmer.

344
00:17:35,280 --> 00:17:41,960
The ST-Link connects to the computer via USB.

345
00:17:39,200 --> 00:17:44,240
Now, which four pins are these?

346
00:17:41,960 --> 00:17:46,280
First, note that the ST-Link has two rows.

347
00:17:44,240 --> 00:17:47,960
There is an upper row and a lower row.

348
00:17:46,280 --> 00:17:52,360
We are only concerned with the lower row.

349
00:17:47,960 --> 00:17:55,159
We do not use the upper row for this connection.

350
00:17:52,360 --> 00:17:59,960
On the lower row, we only need four connections.

351
00:17:55,159 --> 00:18:02,760
We need four wires from the Blue Pill to the ST-Link.

352
00:17:59,960 --> 00:18:05,640
One is 3.3V, which is connected as follows.

353
00:18:02,760 --> 00:18:07,360
It connects to the 3.3V pin on the Blue Pill.

354
00:18:05,640 --> 00:18:11,840
One is GND, which is connected this way.

355
00:18:07,360 --> 00:18:14,559
One is the clock, which connects to the clock pin.

356
00:18:11,840 --> 00:18:17,679
The pin is labeled CLK.

357
00:18:14,559 --> 00:18:19,799
And one is the data line.

358
00:18:17,679 --> 00:18:23,120
It connects to the data pin, labeled DATA.

359
00:18:19,799 --> 00:18:25,720
So these are the four connections.

360
00:18:23,120 --> 00:18:28,200
In this way, the two very important hardware tools

361
00:18:25,720 --> 00:18:31,120
we need for STM32 projects

362
00:18:28,200 --> 00:18:34,480
must be purchased and used in our projects.

363
00:18:31,120 --> 00:18:37,640
There is also a power section on the board.

364
00:18:34,480 --> 00:18:40,840
For example, there is 3.3V.

365
00:18:37,640 --> 00:18:43,080
For projects that do not draw much current,

366
00:18:40,840 --> 00:18:45,840
we can use this 3.3V supply.

367
00:18:43,080 --> 00:18:48,799
There is also a 5V supply here

368
00:18:45,840 --> 00:18:51,799
if we need 5V.

369
00:18:48,799 --> 00:18:53,480
You can use this 5V supply,

370
00:18:51,799 --> 00:18:57,280
but be careful: do not connect motors

371
00:18:53,480 --> 00:18:58,880
or other high-current loads to it.

372
00:18:57,280 --> 00:19:01,559
Use a separate, stable 5V power supply

373
00:18:58,880 --> 00:19:03,799
for those loads.

374
00:19:01,559 --> 00:19:05,520
If too much current is drawn from this board,

375
00:19:03,799 --> 00:19:08,559
you can definitely burn the circuit.

376
00:19:05,520 --> 00:19:12,120
Keep this in mind.

377
00:19:08,559 --> 00:19:14,640

378
00:19:12,120 --> 00:19:19,039
There is another very important point that many people get wrong.

379
00:19:14,640 --> 00:19:21,960
The CubeMX software that we use in our setup

380
00:19:19,039 --> 00:19:24,480
and simulations helps make our work faster and more organized.

381
00:19:21,960 --> 00:19:27,320
Some people say it is the programming itself,

382
00:19:24,480 --> 00:19:30,080
but that is not correct.

383
00:19:27,320 --> 00:19:32,960
CubeMX does not do your programming for you.

384
00:19:30,080 --> 00:19:34,760
It does not do your hardware work for you either.

385
00:19:32,960 --> 00:19:37,960
It simply makes your work faster and more organized.

386
00:19:34,760 --> 00:19:41,679
You still have to work hard on the programming part

387
00:19:37,960 --> 00:19:45,000
to write a good program.

388
00:19:41,679 --> 00:19:47,799
CubeMX generates some register configurations

389
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:49,600
related to I/O and peripherals

390
00:19:47,799 --> 00:19:52,200
and generates the corresponding code

391
00:19:49,600 --> 00:19:54,440
inside the software, as I will explain later.

392
00:19:52,200 --> 00:19:57,840

393
00:19:54,440 --> 00:20:00,320
It generates the code for the configurations you select.

394
00:19:57,840 --> 00:20:02,559

395
00:20:00,320 --> 00:20:05,039
Now let's look at the software tools used in this course.

396
00:20:02,559 --> 00:20:08,720
We use Keil as the IDE.

397
00:20:05,039 --> 00:20:11,919
When you configure things in CubeMX,

398
00:20:08,720 --> 00:20:13,520
it generates code for the project.

399
00:20:11,919 --> 00:20:16,480
That code is then placed into Keil.

400
00:20:13,520 --> 00:20:19,159
Here you can see the project in Keil.

401
00:20:16,480 --> 00:20:21,520
It contains the generated code.

402
00:20:19,159 --> 00:20:26,760
Here you can see various sections for GPIO,

403
00:20:21,520 --> 00:20:29,000
interrupts, the clock system,

404
00:20:26,760 --> 00:20:31,200
timers, and other peripherals.

405
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:34,600
For example, these are the GPIO sections.

406
00:20:31,200 --> 00:20:37,640
This is the NVIC section,

407
00:20:34,600 --> 00:20:41,760
and RCC is the clock configuration.

408
00:20:37,640 --> 00:20:44,960
There are also timers

409
00:20:41,760 --> 00:20:47,440
and communication interfaces.

410
00:20:44,960 --> 00:20:49,640
For example, I2C and SPI are here.

411
00:20:47,440 --> 00:20:51,400
When you select one of these options,

412
00:20:49,640 --> 00:20:53,280
its settings become available.

413
00:20:51,400 --> 00:20:55,520
For example, you can configure an interrupt,

414
00:20:53,280 --> 00:20:59,640
or configure a GPIO pin as an output

415
00:20:55,520 --> 00:21:02,520
or as an input.

416
00:20:59,640 --> 00:21:06,840
You select these options through the wizard,

417
00:21:02,520 --> 00:21:08,760
and then you can configure the clock.

418
00:21:06,840 --> 00:21:11,120
After that, you select the clock settings.

419
00:21:08,760 --> 00:21:13,400
I will explain these steps fully in the project.

420
00:21:11,120 --> 00:21:15,240
Once the clock is configured,

421
00:21:13,400 --> 00:21:20,200
we go to Project Manager.

422
00:21:15,240 --> 00:21:22,760
There we create our project.

423
00:21:20,200 --> 00:21:25,240
In general, after doing these steps,

424
00:21:22,760 --> 00:21:28,400
we generate the code.

425
00:21:25,240 --> 00:21:31,360
When we generate the code,

426
00:21:28,400 --> 00:21:34,679
it is transferred directly into Keil.

427
00:21:31,360 --> 00:21:37,360
It may take a little time

428
00:21:34,679 --> 00:21:42,760
for the code to be generated

429
00:21:37,360 --> 00:21:47,520
before we can write our main code inside Keil.

430
00:21:42,760 --> 00:21:51,325
Again, let me emphasize:

431
00:21:47,520 --> 00:21:52,400
CubeMX only makes your work faster and more organized.

432
00:21:51,325 --> 00:21:55,799
[Laughter]

433
00:21:52,400 --> 00:21:58,000
It generates things such as the configurations you selected.

434
00:21:55,799 --> 00:22:00,200
For example, if you configured a pin as an output,

435
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:02,480
or configured the clock system,

436
00:22:00,200 --> 00:22:04,600
CubeMX generates the corresponding code for you.

437
00:22:02,480 --> 00:22:07,279
It creates this code

438
00:22:04,600 --> 00:22:12,760
and places it inside Keil.

439
00:22:07,279 --> 00:22:15,400
But it does not write your entire program for you.

440
00:22:12,760 --> 00:22:17,919
And it does not complete your hardware either.

441
00:22:15,400 --> 00:22:19,799
That's not what it does.

442
00:22:17,919 --> 00:22:22,240
Here we have Open Project,

443
00:22:19,799 --> 00:22:24,640
which I will explain later when we build a project.

444
00:22:22,240 --> 00:22:26,320
For now, I just want you to understand

445
00:22:24,640 --> 00:22:29,240
what CubeMX and Keil are.

446
00:22:26,320 --> 00:22:30,960
This is the Keil environment you are seeing.

447
00:22:29,240 --> 00:22:36,120
I already have a program written here.

448
00:22:30,960 --> 00:22:38,600
As you can see, the things we configured in CubeMX

449
00:22:36,120 --> 00:22:40,720
have been generated here.

450
00:22:38,600 --> 00:22:43,240
For example, we configured a pin here,

451
00:22:40,720 --> 00:22:45,000
and CubeMX generated the corresponding code.

452
00:22:43,240 --> 00:22:47,440
For example, we configured another GPIO pin,

453
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:49,159
and its code appears here as well.

454
00:22:47,440 --> 00:22:51,200
So CubeMX generated these parts of the code.

455
00:22:49,159 --> 00:22:54,679
But it did not write the entire program for you.

456
00:22:51,200 --> 00:22:58,880
Keep this in mind.

457
00:22:54,679 --> 00:23:01,880
This is the Keil environment.

458
00:22:58,880 --> 00:23:04,240
One of the software tools used in the course is Keil.

459
00:23:01,880 --> 00:23:07,960
In Keil, we write our program.

460
00:23:04,240 --> 00:23:11,080
For example, we write code inside the while loop

461
00:23:07,960 --> 00:23:13,360
or anywhere else in the program.

462
00:23:11,080 --> 00:23:15,159
Then we edit it

463
00:23:13,360 --> 00:23:19,279
and build the project.

464
00:23:15,159 --> 00:23:21,840
We use the Build function to compile it.

465
00:23:19,279 --> 00:23:26,080
The things we do in Keil

466
00:23:21,840 --> 00:23:28,440
and the things we configure in STM32CubeMX

467
00:23:26,080 --> 00:23:31,600
are different: in CubeMX we configure

468
00:23:28,440 --> 00:23:33,360
peripherals, clocks, and other hardware settings.

469
00:23:31,600 --> 00:23:35,600
As I explained, in this section

470
00:23:33,360 --> 00:23:39,279
we configure the peripherals.

471
00:23:35,600 --> 00:23:41,880
For example, GPIO pins can be configured,

472
00:23:39,279 --> 00:23:44,000
and you can enable a timer or I2C.

473
00:23:41,880 --> 00:23:46,720
You can enable whichever peripheral you need.

474
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:49,720
You can configure the clock here as well.

475
00:23:46,720 --> 00:23:54,520
These are the things we do in CubeMX.

476
00:23:49,720 --> 00:23:58,080
The programmer is the ST-Link,

477
00:23:54,520 --> 00:24:00,159
which I showed you earlier.

478
00:23:58,080 --> 00:24:03,400
It transfers the program from Keil

479
00:24:00,159 --> 00:24:06,600
to our microcontroller.

480
00:24:03,400 --> 00:24:09,320
There is also the STM32 package.

481
00:24:06,600 --> 00:24:12,559
We need to use certain packages.

482
00:24:09,320 --> 00:24:14,880
What are these packages useful for?

483
00:24:12,559 --> 00:24:17,120
I will provide the required package to you.

484
00:24:14,880 --> 00:24:20,200
Here is a package that I have used.

485
00:24:17,120 --> 00:24:22,760
For example, this is the STM32F1 package.

486
00:24:20,200 --> 00:24:29,120
Many peripherals and libraries

487
00:24:22,760 --> 00:24:31,720
that we do not need to write from scratch

488
00:24:29,120 --> 00:24:34,159
are included in this package.

489
00:24:31,720 --> 00:24:37,520
The company provides this package for us.

490
00:24:34,159 --> 00:24:40,840
Many peripherals and libraries

491
00:24:37,520 --> 00:24:42,880
are already included.

492
00:24:40,840 --> 00:24:46,679
There is no need to rewrite everything from scratch.

493
00:24:42,880 --> 00:24:48,840
The company has provided these packages

494
00:24:46,679 --> 00:24:50,880
for us to use.

495
00:24:48,840 --> 00:24:53,279
I will also provide them to you on my website

496
00:24:50,880 --> 00:24:55,760
so that you can use them.

497
00:24:53,279 --> 00:24:58,360
This way, you do not have to write all those libraries,

498
00:24:55,760 --> 00:25:00,799
headers, and includes from scratch.

499
00:24:58,360 --> 00:25:03,919
They are all included in the package.

500
00:25:00,799 --> 00:25:09,960
We can simply use them.

501
00:25:03,919 --> 00:25:12,960
So these are the software tools used in the course.

502
00:25:09,960 --> 00:25:15,960
Now let's look at the overall process from code to real execution.

503
00:25:12,960 --> 00:25:19,600
Here is the general flow.

504
00:25:15,960 --> 00:25:22,600
We write our code in Keil,

505
00:25:19,600 --> 00:25:25,240
and then we build it there.

506
00:25:22,600 --> 00:25:27,440
Then it is ready to be programmed.

507
00:25:25,240 --> 00:25:29,760
Programmed into what?

508
00:25:27,440 --> 00:25:34,919
When we build the program in Keil,

509
00:25:29,760 --> 00:25:37,600
it is placed into the Flash memory.

510
00:25:34,919 --> 00:25:39,840

511
00:25:37,600 --> 00:25:44,840
That is our permanent memory.

512
00:25:39,840 --> 00:25:47,039
Then we program it and perform a hardware test.

513
00:25:44,840 --> 00:25:49,960
For example, we test turning the LED on and off

514
00:25:47,039 --> 00:25:52,279
on our breadboard.

515
00:25:49,960 --> 00:25:57,120
We perform the hardware test.

516
00:25:52,279 --> 00:25:59,600

517
00:25:57,120 --> 00:26:01,440
And this is the final part of the process.

518
00:25:59,600 --> 00:26:03,960
In CubeMX, to create our first project,

519
00:26:01,440 --> 00:26:06,200
which I will demonstrate in the next session,

520
00:26:03,960 --> 00:26:08,799
we first need to configure STM32 in CubeMX.

521
00:26:06,200 --> 00:26:10,279
We configure the GPIO,

522
00:26:08,799 --> 00:26:12,520
for example, setting one pin as an output

523
00:26:10,279 --> 00:26:15,000
so that it can turn the LED on and off.

524
00:26:12,520 --> 00:26:17,120
Then we configure the clock.

525
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:19,200
We can use the internal clock,

526
00:26:17,120 --> 00:26:21,919
an external clock,

527
00:26:19,200 --> 00:26:25,320
or the crystal on the Blue Pill.

528
00:26:21,919 --> 00:26:28,039
Then we generate the code.

529
00:26:25,320 --> 00:26:31,720
The generated code goes into the software

530
00:26:28,039 --> 00:26:34,559
where the project is built.

531
00:26:31,720 --> 00:26:36,559
Then the program is compiled,

532
00:26:34,559 --> 00:26:39,919
and the program we wrote in Keil

533
00:26:36,559 --> 00:26:42,799
is programmed into the STM32 microcontroller

534
00:26:39,919 --> 00:26:45,760
on the Blue Pill.

535
00:26:42,799 --> 00:26:48,799
It is stored in its Flash memory,

536
00:26:45,760 --> 00:26:52,440
and the LED then turns on and off.

537
00:26:48,799 --> 00:26:56,320
Okay, so in the first session we learned the general concepts.

538
00:26:52,440 --> 00:27:00,559
These concepts help us understand

539
00:26:56,320 --> 00:27:04,600
what we are using and how we are using it.

540
00:27:00,559 --> 00:27:06,919
The important point is to understand

541
00:27:04,600 --> 00:27:09,640
what we are working with and how it works.

542
00:27:06,919 --> 00:27:12,320
When you fully understand the concepts,

543
00:27:09,640 --> 00:27:16,240
